  "final_message": "<think>Interesting \u2014 the lock file has hashes for gunicorn 26.2.0 but the environment's index serves a different wheel hash (the local pip download shows hash bd249d... which IS in the lock file). Wait, the error says the downloaded archive's hash was bd249d0b3f7972f7432f0a6b6ff3b3ee2d129f70cd1ff6c09a9dd9e29a2b88e3 \u2014 hmm, actually it lists the hash in the message: \"gunicorn==26.2.0 --hash=sha256:bd249d0b3f7972f7432f0a6b6ff3b3ee2d129f70cd1ff6c09a9dd9e29a2b88e3\". And the lock file has both hashes listed for gunicorn: 62b8648... and bd249d0b... So the hash matches one of the two!\n\nWait, the error says hashes are missing for some requirements. This happens in --require-hashes mode when a package is required *without* hashes in the constraints file... Actually the issue: the constraints file (requirements.lock) lists gunicorn with hashes; the requirements.txt lists `gunicorn==26.2.0` without hashes. In hash-checking mode, pip requires that ALL requirements (including those in the -r file) have hashes \u2014 the constraints file's hashes don't count for the direct requirement? Hmm.\n\nActually pip's --require-hashes mode: every requirement must have a hash. When a package is listed in requirements.txt without hashes but constrained by requirements.lock with hashes... The error message shows that pip found the download's hash matches bd249d... (which is in the lock). Hmm, but the error is about \"hashes are missing for some requirements\". This is a known pip behavior: hashes in a constraints file are NOT used to satisfy the hash requirement for the primary requirement. Actually no \u2014 pip docs say: \"constraints files... hashes in constraints files are not used for hash-checking mode\"? Let me think.\n\npip's docs: \"If you are using hashes in a constraints file, they will not be used to validate the requirements.\" Actually the pip docs on hash-checking mode state that hashes must be specified for all requirements including those from constraint files. Hmm.\n\nBut the important question: does this fail in the Docker build? The Docker build runs exactly:\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock\n```\nThis is the pre-existing setup in the workspace \u2014 presumably it worked before (the base project was given as working). But my local dry-run just failed! Let me check whether the failure is due to my local pip version (25.1.1) vs the Docker image's pip (python:3.13-slim ships pip 25.x too, probably 25.0.x or 25.1).\n\nHmm wait. Maybe the failure is because flask is already installed locally (satisfied) \u2014 no. The error is about gunicorn's hashes. Since flask was \"already satisfied\", pip didn't need to verify its hash. For gunicorn, it downloaded the wheel and computed the hash: bd249d0b3f7972f7432f0a6b6ff3b3ee2d129f70cd1ff6c09a9dd9e29a2b88e3. The lock file for gunicorn lists:\n```\ngunicorn==26.2.0 \\\n    --hash=sha256:62b864895d9ebff0b2f9867ba04fe811c93121596540830c9c916d0769668447 \\\n    --hash=sha256:bd249d0b3f7972f7432f0a6b6ff3b3ee2d129f70cd1ff6c09a9dd9e29a2b88e3\n```\nSo the hash IS available. But pip's error says hashes are missing for the requirement... \n\nAh, I understand now: in hash-checking mode, pip requires hashes to be attached to the *requirement* being installed. When a requirement comes from a constraints file, pip doesn't propagate the constraint's hashes to the requirement. So `gunicorn==26.2.0` from requirements.txt has no hashes \u2192 error.\n\nBut then how did the original Docker build work? Maybe it didn't get to that point, or maybe the base image's pip behaves differently, or maybe the sandbox's PyPI proxy serves a different artifact set than the real PyPI.\n\nHmm, wait. The two gunicorn hashes: one is for the wheel and one for the sdist. The message says \"the following requirements have missing hashes\"... Let me re-read the actual pip output more carefully by running it again with full output.\n\nLet me test in a fresh venv to avoid the \"already satisfied\" path, and capture the whole error.\n\nActually, this matters: if the Docker build fails, the app won't deploy. But note the workspace came with this lock file pre-generated, and the README says \"Dependencies are pinned: backend/requirements.lock holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file.\" So the intended flow is exactly what the Dockerfile does. If it's broken in the sandbox's pip version, that's an environment quirk. But I should verify and, if needed, fix it (e.g., add hashes to requirements.txt or use `--require-hashes` properly).\n\nSince I can't run Docker, I should at least ensure the pip command works with the sandbox's pip (which likely matches the Docker image's pip: python:3.13-slim ships pip 25.x; the sandbox has 25.1.1).\n\nLet me investigate the exact failure ina venv.</think>\n\n",
